<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Use translated concept answers, distinguish recodes from column renames, and harmonise two different site vocabularies.
</div>

This chapter uses the synthetic questionnaire dataset described in [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run the sections in order in one R session. All printed results below were computed
with dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.5. They are teaching data, not clinical findings.

Concept selections refer to the searches and saved definitions in
[chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html), including the separate questionnaire
catalogue. Additional body-measurement concepts are found on screen in the
[formula walkthrough](book-15-formulas.html#find-the-inputs-before-calculating).

## Start this chapter

Start a fresh R session in the `book-work` directory created in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#get-the-book-helpers). Run this setup first:

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/fixture.R")
practice <- book_fixture(categories = TRUE)
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

The `site` connection is ready. This chapter creates its temporary questionnaire fixture locally.

## Translation follows the value type

A concept-valued answer is a category, not a quantity to average. With the recipe API,
the declared value column supplies that type. Your variable name does not need
the suffix `_concept_id`.

```{r category-variable}
education <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
class(education)
```

The event selects the education question; the reducer reads its last coded answer.
Selecting the question concept as the value would answer a different question.

```{r default-translation}
categories <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
book_symbols(recipe_execute(categories, out = c(people = "D_categories"), conns = conns))
```

Translation is the default. The data values become labelled factors on the server.

```{r default-table}
ds.omop.table("D_categories", "education", conns = conns)
```

You see category names and released counts. The concept IDs used in the definition
are vocabulary identifiers; they do not appear as numeric answers in this table.

## Recode answers to yes and no

An explicit label map changes categories by concept identity. It does not search
for text in each site's database.

```{r recode}
fever <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900002L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"),
  labels = c("900301" = "yes", "900302" = "no"))
class(fever)
```

Only the declared answer concepts receive those labels. A missing answer stays
missing; absence of a record is not a clinical “no”.

```{r recode-run}
answers <- omop_recipe(variables = list(fever = fever),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
book_symbols(recipe_execute(answers, out = c(people = "D_category_answers"), conns = conns))
```

```{r recode-table}
recode_table_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_category_answers$fever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(recode_table_result))
```

<details><summary>Full aggregate response</summary>

```{r recode-table-full}
recode_table_result
```

</details>

This real ds.table call excludes missing answers and shows the supplied labels.
`ds.omop.table()` additionally retains the complete published category contract,
including declared zero levels, and handles punctuation in labels.

## Rename the column separately

A column name identifies the variable; a label names one of its categories.
Name an explicit variable in the recipe list, without changing what its answers mean.

```{r column-rename}
renamed <- omop_recipe(variables = list(schooling = education),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
book_symbols(recipe_execute(renamed, out = c(people = "D_renamed_categories"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_renamed_categories", datasources = conns)
```

The list name supplies the scalar variable name. A concept label map would instead
change values inside that column. The visit walkthrough uses a value-preserving
mutate alias when adding a new name to an already assigned date column.

```{r renamed-table}
ds.omop.table("D_renamed_categories", "schooling", conns = conns)
```

The education categories and counts are unchanged. Next we address differences
between sites, where even the vocabulary names can vary.

## Rename automatically generated concept columns

Some layouts expand one event into separate columns for each concept. A panel
of inpatient/outpatient counts is one example. `column_names` targets their
stable generated keys, not the explicit scalar variable from the previous step.

```{r generated-rename}
visit_event <- omop_event("visit_occurrence", c(9201L, 9202L), name = "visits")
visit_population <- omop_population("visitors", index = omop_index(visit_event))
renamed_panel <- omop_recipe(events = list(visit_event),
  populations = list(visit_population),
  column_names = c(visits_9201_count = "admissions", visits_9202_count = "outpatient"),
  outputs = omop_output("panel", rows = "visitors", format = "person_period",
    options = list(sources = list(visit_event), bins = list(width = 30L),
      window = omop_window(0, 29), analyses = "count")))
class(renamed_panel)
```

The keys combine the source name, concept identity and analysis. They remain
stable when a vocabulary changes its wording; your chosen column names remain
readable. An explicit name cannot collide with a protected or another final column.

```{r generated-run}
book_symbols(recipe_execute(renamed_panel, out = c(panel = "D_renamed_panel"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_renamed_panel", datasources = conns)
```

The resulting count columns have your declared names. Using `column_names` with
an explicit scalar name such as `education` would not rename that scalar column.

## Find and save the category identities

For the next examples, the questionnaire helper includes a small synthetic
classification catalogue. Its teaching ATC and ICD-10 entries illustrate the
hierarchy structure; they are not clinical mappings to reuse in a study.
Search the catalogue before choosing an order or a rollup.

```{r category-discovery}
catalogue <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, standard_only = FALSE, limit = 100,
  conns = conns)$pooled
knitr::kable(catalogue[c("concept_name", "vocabulary_id", "concept_class_id", "standard_concept")])
ids <- setNames(catalogue$concept_id, catalogue$concept_name)
education_ids <- ids[c("Primary education", "Secondary education", "University education")]
education_keys <- paste0("concept:", education_ids)
education_set <- omop_concept_set(unname(education_ids))
category_file <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(list(answers = education_set), category_file)
identical(readRDS(category_file)$answers, education_set)
```

The question and answer concepts were located in observation in
[chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html#find-the-questionnaire-questions-answers-and-causes).
Primary education is declared in this catalogue but is not an observed answer.
A complete public category domain can include an unused level.

## Put primary, secondary and tertiary in order

Alphabetical order is not educational order. Declare stable concept identities,
then give the three levels the short labels used by the study.

```{r category-order}
education_ordered <- omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(
  levels = education_keys, order = omop_category_order(education_keys), ordered = TRUE))
ordered_recipe <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = education_ordered),
  labels = setNames(c("primary", "secondary", "tertiary"), education_ids))
ordered_result <- recipe_execute(ordered_recipe, out = "D_ordered", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_ordered", "education", conns = conns)
```

The table follows primary < secondary < tertiary, including the declared zero
level. The ordering is a protocol assertion, not a rank inferred from frequency.
Ordered factors use polynomial contrasts; set `ordered = FALSE` if your model
needs nominal treatment contrasts instead. A treatment reference and display
order are separate choices.

**Exercise.** Check the actual type of this column.

<details><summary>Solution: an ordered category</summary>

```{r category-order-type}
dsBaseClient::ds.class("D_ordered$education", datasources = conns)
```

It is an ordered factor, not the integers used internally to store a factor.

</details>

## Choose labels and language

`name` displays the concept name. `code_name` adds its vocabulary code; it does
not expose numeric concept IDs as answers. A language declaration requests the
vocabulary's synonyms for that language, falling back to a concept name when
none is available. It does not translate free text automatically.

```{r category-language}
language_id <- ids[["English language"]]
styled <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  code_and_name = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(label_style = "code_name")),
  local_name = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(
    language = omop_category_language(language_id)))))
style_result <- recipe_execute(styled, out = "D_styled", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_styled", "local_name", conns = conns)
```

The teaching vocabulary supplies “Secondary schooling” and “Tertiary schooling”
as English synonyms. The category identity is unchanged by these labels.

**Exercise.** Display the code-and-name variant from the same extraction.

<details><summary>Solution: a second label style</summary>

```{r category-style-table}
ds.omop.table("D_styled", "code_and_name", conns = conns)
```

Use `code` or `vocabulary_code` when that public coding context is needed.
`source_code` is refused on this route because source-code disclosure needs a
separately admitted policy. Prefer names for an ordinary analysis table.

</details>

## Keep Missing and Unknown distinct

Missing means there is no usable value; Unknown represents OMOP concept zero.
Neither means a recorded “no”. The missing policy can make each an explicit
factor level without inventing a clinical answer.

```{r category-missing}
missing_recipe <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  education = omop_category(education, omop_category_spec(
    missing = omop_category_missing(na = "level", unknown = "level"))),
  sex = omop_category(omop_variable(name = "sex", table = "person", column = "gender_concept_id"), omop_category_spec(
    missing = omop_category_missing(na = "level", unknown = "level")))))
missing_result <- recipe_execute(missing_recipe, out = "D_missing_categories", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_missing_categories", "education", conns = conns)
```

Missing answers are now visible as Missing. The public Unknown level may have
zero count; declaring it does not assert that anyone has an unknown answer.
The sex column contains FEMALE and MALE; its declared Missing and Unknown levels
remain empty.

**Exercise.** Check which declared sex levels are actually observed.

<details><summary>Solution: declared and observed levels</summary>

```{r category-unknown}
ds.omop.table("D_missing_categories", "sex", conns = conns)
```

The two observed levels each contain twelve people. A declared Unknown level
does not turn an observed FEMALE or MALE value into an unknown value.

</details>

## Present a semantic flag as an integer, logical or factor

A binary reducer asks whether an event occurred. It has false/true meaning before
presentation, so it can safely become 0/1, FALSE/TRUE or named categories.

```{r category-binary}
has_visit <- omop_variable(event = visit_event, reducer = omop_reduce("binary"))
flags <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  integer_flag = has_visit,
  logical_flag = omop_category(has_visit, omop_category_spec(binary = omop_category_binary("logical"))),
  named_flag = omop_category(has_visit, omop_category_spec(binary = omop_category_binary("factor")))))
flag_result <- recipe_execute(flags, out = "D_flags", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_flags", "named_flag", conns = conns)
```

This “no” means no selected visit in the defined history. It does not mean a
negative answer to the fever question. Binary presentation is for semantic flags,
not an arbitrary two-level concept column.

**Exercise.** Inspect the published types without reading patient rows.

<details><summary>Solution: three representations</summary>

```{r category-binary-types}
flag_columns <- recipe_codebook(flag_result)[[1]]$columns
knitr::kable(flag_columns[c("name", "r_type")])
```

</details>

## Roll up drug classes and cause chapters

A rollup follows vocabulary relationships. ATC levels are classification classes,
not a number of ancestor steps. ICD-10 chapters require the source coding
relationships; a similar-looking code prefix is insufficient.

```{r category-hierarchy-discovery}
drug_id <- ids[["Practice medicine"]]
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.ancestors(drug_id, conns = conns)$pooled)
icd_id <- ids[["Teaching circulatory code"]]
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.relationships(icd_id, relationship_id = "Maps to", conns = conns)$pooled)
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.relationships(icd_id, relationship_id = "Is a", conns = conns)$pooled)
knitr::kable(ds.omop.concept.locate(c(drug_id, ids[["Myocardial infarction"]]),
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled)
classification_sets <- list(drug = omop_concept_set(drug_id),
  causes = omop_concept_set(unname(ids[c("Myocardial infarction", "Pneumonia")])))
saveRDS(classification_sets, category_file)
```

Now choose the public domain to be classified. Ambiguous targets fail by default;
resolve them in the study protocol with explicit priority or multiple membership.
Do not resolve ambiguity by inspecting patient frequencies.

```{r category-rollups}
drug_value <- omop_category_reduce(
  omop_category_rows(omop_event("drug_exposure", classification_sets$drug), "concept_id"),
  omop_reduce("last_value"))
cause_value <- omop_category_reduce(
  omop_category_rows(omop_event("death"), "concept_id"), omop_reduce("first_value"))
rollups <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  atc_first = omop_category(drug_value, omop_category_spec(rollup =
    omop_category_rollup("atc", level = 1, domain = classification_sets$drug))),
  atc_second = omop_category(drug_value, omop_category_spec(rollup =
    omop_category_rollup("atc", level = 2, domain = classification_sets$drug))),
  chapter = omop_category(cause_value, omop_category_spec(rollup =
    omop_category_rollup("icd10", level = "chapter", vocabulary = "ICD10",
      domain = classification_sets$causes)))))
rollup_result <- recipe_execute(rollups, out = "D_rollups", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table("D_rollups", "chapter", conns = conns)
```

The cause counts are now grouped by the synthetic chapter relationships.
The underlying person roster is unchanged. Use a validated clinical vocabulary
for a real cause-of-death classification.

**Exercise.** Compare the two ATC levels in the public codebook.

<details><summary>Solution: levels come from vocabulary classes</summary>

```{r category-atc-codebook}
rollup_columns <- recipe_codebook(rollup_result)[[1]]$columns
lapply(rollup_columns$levels[match(c("atc_first", "atc_second"), rollup_columns$name)],
  function(x) x$labels)
```

The first and second levels have different labels even though the same drug
records contribute to both. Neither level is selected by counting observed drugs.

</details>

## Read the codebook of each table

Keep the execution result: it carries the admitted table metadata. Calling
`recipe_codebook()` on it is local and does not query patient rows. Calling it
on the recipe instead produces a prospective schema, with unresolved fields
marked explicitly.

```{r category-codebook}
book <- recipe_codebook(ordered_result)
print(book[[1]][c("status", "row_grain", "row_keys")])
knitr::kable(book[[1]]$columns[c("stable_key", "name", "semantic_type", "r_type",
  "unit", "privacy_class", "missing_policy")])
```

The stable key identifies the column before a display rename. Semantic type tells
you what a value means; R type tells you how it is represented. Units, category
levels, ordering, reference, contrasts and derivation belong with the analysis
protocol. None of these fields is a count or a missingness estimate.

## Open two practice connections

The other lessons use one server. This check uses two DSLite
connections. Both synthetic databases hold the same answer IDs. The second
vocabulary calls University education “University qualification” and declares
a newer vocabulary version. No patient records are downloaded.

```{r two-connections}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
pair <- book_fixture(two_sites = TRUE)
two_conns <- pair$conns
names(two_conns)
```

Each name represents a separate DataSHIELD session with its own private table.

## See the vocabulary difference

```{r two-vocabularies}
lookup <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(900102L, conns = two_conns)
lapply(lookup$per_site, function(x) x["concept_name"])
```

This is permitted vocabulary metadata, not observed answer frequencies.

## Declare a shared label

```{r harmonised}
harmonised <- omop_recipe(variables = list(education = omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"),
  labels = c("900102" = "University education"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
book_symbols(recipe_execute(harmonised, out = c(people = "D_shared"), conns = two_conns))
```

A common explicit label resolves the known wording difference. Harmonisation is
based on concept identity and the published label contract, not on matching
patient records or guessing that similar words mean the same thing.

## Compare released category sets

```{r site-tables}
site_tables <- lapply(names(two_conns), function(site)
  ds.omop.table("D_shared", "education", conns = two_conns[site]))
names(site_tables) <- names(two_conns)
site_tables
```

```{r identical-categories}
identical(dimnames(site_tables[[1]]), dimnames(site_tables[[2]]))
```

Both real executions returned the same category set. Now combine their counts:

```{r pooled-categories}
ds.omop.table("D_shared", "education", conns = two_conns)
```

The pooled counts add released cells. They do not link people across sites.
In a real network, confirm the concept meanings as well as their labels.

## Try it

Confirm that the shared definition is a recipe, then close both temporary connections.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>


```{r solution}
class(harmonised)
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = two_conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(two_conns)
unlink(pair$path)
```

</details>

## Exercise 2: a common variant

Does a label map change a concept set? Inspect the recipe object locally, compare
its selection with the original education variable, and resolve the answer
identities using the vocabulary lookup already returned above.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-2}
selected <- harmonised$variables$education
without_labels <- selected
without_labels$labels <- NULL
label_ids <- as.integer(names(selected$labels))
answer_names <- lookup$per_site[[1L]]
knitr::kable(data.frame(
  Answer = answer_names$concept_name[match(label_ids, answer_names$concept_id)],
  Shared_label = unname(selected$labels)))
identical(selected$event$concepts, education$event$concepts)
identical(selected$event$concepts, without_labels$event$concepts)
```

The label map names the University education answer. Removing that map leaves
the observation selection identical (`TRUE`): it changes the displayed answer
label, not which question is selected. Concept meaning still requires study review.

</details>

## Exercise 3: a pitfall to avoid

Check that the recipe remains serialisable after harmonisation.

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-3}
is.character(recipe_export_json(harmonised))
```

TRUE means this definition can be recorded. It does not prove that two different concepts are clinically equivalent.

</details>
## Recap

Concept values translate by default. Label maps recode answers; column maps rename variables. Shared labels give common categories across vocabulary wording differences, after you confirm concept meanings.

**Next:** [11. Reusable studies and standalone verbs](book-11-parameters.html).
